
=== SQL Database in Microsoft Fabric

ifndef::pro[]

SQL Database in Microsoft Fabric is only supported in the professional version of SymmetricDS.

endif::pro[]

ifdef::pro[]

SQL Database in Microsoft Fabric is supported as a source and as a target.  SymmetricDS captures changes in it with database triggers, see
<<Capturing Changes>>.
It uses the https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/connect/jdbc/download-microsoft-jdbc-driver-for-sql-server[Microsoft JDBC driver].

==== Setup

Set up the node by using the <<Set Up New Replication,Configure Diagram>> or the <<Add Node,Connect Database>> wizard and selecting SQL Database in
Microsoft Fabric as the type.  Use the full host name of the database for the endpoint and the name of the database, including the generated suffix,
for the database name. The following is an example of the JDBC URL:

----
jdbc:sqlserver://<workspace>.database.fabric.microsoft.com:1433;database={<database_name>-<guid>};encrypt=true;trustServerCertificate=false;authentication=ActiveDirectoryServicePrincipal
----

The SQL Database in Microsoft Fabric accepts only Microsoft Entra ID authentication.  SymmetricDS uses the service principal of an Entra app registration.
The credentials are entered in the User Id and Password fields:

[horizontal]
User Id:: The Application (client) ID of the app registration
Password:: A client secret of the app registration

WARNING: The connection string shown by the Fabric portal uses `Authentication=ActiveDirectoryInteractive`.  This mode needs to open a browser for
sign-in whenever it opens a connection, which is not compatible with SymmetricDS.  Use `Authentication=ActiveDirectoryServicePrincipal` instead.

The `fabric` module contains the Microsoft Authentication Library that is required for Entra ID authentication.  The web console downloads it when
the connection is tested, or you can run `symadmin module install fabric` to install it.

==== Permissions

The app registration needs to be granted access in each of the following steps:

. Create an app registration in Microsoft Entra ID and a client secret for it.  Depending on the tenant settings, service principals might need to be
allowed to use Fabric APIs, which is a tenant setting in the Fabric admin portal.
. Grant the app registration access to the database item in Fabric, using the Share action on the SQL database item (or a workspace role) with at least the Read permission.
. Create a database user for the app registration and add it to the roles that SymmetricDS needs.  Connect to the database as an administrator and run the following
script, replacing `<app registration name>` with the display name of the app registration:

[source, Sql]
----
CREATE USER [<app registration name>] FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER;
ALTER ROLE db_ddladmin ADD MEMBER [<app registration name>];
ALTER ROLE db_datareader ADD MEMBER [<app registration name>];
ALTER ROLE db_datawriter ADD MEMBER [<app registration name>];
----

These roles are enough for SymmetricDS to create its tables, functions, and triggers (including the database-level trigger that captures changes to
the structure of tables), capture changes, and load data.

==== Capturing Changes

SymmetricDS captures changes in SQL Database in Microsoft Fabric with database triggers.  When adding the node, the capture type is trigger-based by default.
Time-based capture is also available, but log mining is not.

Changes to data (inserts, updates, and deletes) are always captured.  Changes to the structure of tables are captured only when the
`trigger.capture.ddl.changes` parameter is set to `true`, see <<Capturing Schema Changes>>.

The triggers on tables run as the user that changes the table (`EXECUTE AS CALLER`).  A user or application that changes the data of a captured table needs
permission on that table only.  No permission on the SymmetricDS tables is needed.  A user or application that connects to the database also needs the Read
permission on the database item, as described in the Permissions section above.

IMPORTANT: Keep the `mssql.trigger.execute.as` parameter set to `caller`, which is the default.  SQL Database in Microsoft Fabric does not support
`EXECUTE AS` with `self` or `owner`, and creating the triggers fails with error 22607.

==== Capturing Schema Changes

SymmetricDS can capture changes to the structure of tables and replicate them to other nodes, as it does for SQL Server.  Set the
`trigger.capture.ddl.changes` parameter to `true` and restart the node.  SymmetricDS then creates a database-level trigger that records the statements
that create, alter, or drop tables, indexes, views, functions, stored procedures, triggers, types, and schemas.  The statements are sent to the target nodes
and run there.

When a SQL Database in Microsoft Fabric is the source of a mirror, which is set up with the Mirror button of the <<Set Up New Replication,Configure Diagram>>,
SymmetricDS sets the parameters for you and replicates both the data and the structure of the tables.  See <<Set Up New Replication>> for the databases
that can be used as the target.

By default, the `trigger.capture.ddl.check.trigger.hist` parameter is `true`.  Statements that create, alter, or drop a table, or an index or trigger on a
table, are then captured only for tables that SymmetricDS already captures, so a statement that creates a new table is not captured.  Statements for views,
functions, stored procedures, types, and schemas are captured in both modes.  Set the parameter to `false` to capture the statements for every table in the
database, so that new tables are replicated.  A mirror requires this setting, and the console sets it for you.

A statement that SymmetricDS runs on this database while it loads changes from another node is recorded with the name of that node and is not sent back
to it, so two nodes can replicate changes to the structure of tables in both directions.

The database-level trigger runs as the user that runs the statement.  A user or application that creates, alters, or drops a table, index, view, function,
stored procedure, trigger, type, or schema in this database while schema changes are captured needs permission to read the `SYM_TRIGGER_HIST` and
`SYM_TRIGGER` tables and to insert into the `SYM_DATA` table.  The `db_datareader` and `db_datawriter` roles that SymmetricDS uses include these permissions.
Without them, the statement fails with error 373.

==== Bulk Loading

SymmetricDS can bulk load SQL Database in Microsoft Fabric with the bulk copy API of the Microsoft JDBC driver.  When a channel uses the bulk data
loader, which the reload channel does by default, SymmetricDS writes the rows of a batch to a staging file and streams them to the database in groups of
10,000 rows.  The rows are copied in the same transaction as the SymmetricDS batch, so a failed batch doesn't leave part of its rows in the table.

No extra setup is needed.  The bulk copy uses the same connection and service principal as the rest of SymmetricDS, and nothing is uploaded to OneLake.
Values of identity columns are loaded as they are sent, and empty strings stay distinct from null values.

==== Known Limitations

===== Unicode Text

By default, SymmetricDS captures text as non-Unicode.  Characters that are not in the code page of the database collation are affected as follows:

* In `NCHAR`, `NVARCHAR`, `NTEXT`, and `NVARCHAR(MAX)` columns, the characters are replaced with `?`.
* In `XML` columns, the statement that inserts the value fails with error 6355 because the trigger cannot convert the document, and the row is not saved.

Characters that are in the code page, such as `é` in a Latin1 collation, are captured unchanged.

To capture these columns as Unicode, set the `mssql.use.ntypes.for.sync` parameter to `true`, restart the node, and rebuild the triggers of the tables.

===== Spatial Data Types

Values of `GEOGRAPHY` and `GEOMETRY` columns are captured as text in the well-known text format, without the spatial reference identifier (SRID).  When
SymmetricDS creates a table on a SQL Server target, it creates these columns as `VARCHAR(MAX)`.  `HIERARCHYID` values are captured and loaded as binary
values.

endif::pro[]
